-- title:
--   Whitney decomposition data for a domain, with Dyda's inequality
-- statement:
--   **The Whitney decomposition data for a bounded Lipschitz domain**, as Lemma A.1 of
--   Bux–Kassmann–Schulze quotes it, packaged as a structure.
--
--   The source's proof of Lemma A.1 opens: "The Whitney decomposition technique provides a
--   family $\mathcal B$ of balls with the following properties. (i) There exists $c = c(d)$
--   such that for every $x, y \in \Omega$ with $\lvert x-y\rvert < c\,\mathrm{dist}(x,\partial\Omega)$
--   there exists a ball $B \in \mathcal B$ with $x, y \in B$. (ii) For every $B \in \mathcal B$,
--   $B^* \subseteq \Omega$. (iii) The family $\{B^*\}$ has the finite overlapping property."
--   It then uses Dyda's inequality (13) from [Dyda06]. Neither is proved in the source, and
--   neither is proved here: this structure carries them as data, so every result depending on
--   Lemma A.1 states its dependence in its own type.
--
--   A value of this structure for a domain $\Omega$ supplies a countable family of balls with
--   centres `ctr` and radii `rad`, an overlap bound `overlapBound`, the Dyda constant
--   `dydaConst`, and the three properties as fields — with (ii) and (iii) as stated above, and
--   Dyda's inequality (13) as `dyda`.
--
--   **The constants are attached to this $\Omega$**, matching the source's
--   $\tilde c = \tilde c(d,\kappa,\alpha,\Omega)$. The companion `QFS.WhitneyBallData` instead
--   carries one overlap bound and one Dyda constant serving *every* ball $B_R(x_0)$ at once,
--   which is what the source's "in particular, if $\Omega$ is a ball, the constant can be chosen
--   independently of $\Omega$" asserts. `QFS.exists_whitneyBallData` — the statement that such a
--   family exists for balls — is an `Open` milestone of this mission.
